EXT. SHELLED CHURCHYARD, NORTHERN FRONT - NIGHT

Broken headstones. A fallen bell tower. Snow drifts across the graves like ash. Distant artillery drums on the horizon.

SERGEANT EZEQUIEL "ZEKE" LANDGRAF-OKAFOR (40s, helmet, rifle slung) crouches behind a toppled angel. Beside him, PRIVATE WILHELMINA "WILLA" PASCOE-DIALLO (19, freckles, radio on her back) shivers. A few feet away, CORPORAL TIBOR NAGY-ABERNATHY (30s, a medic's armband) tends a wounded boy who lies unmoving in the snow.

ZEKE
Willa, any word from Battalion?

WILLA
Static, Sarge.

ZEKE
Try again.

WILLA
I've tried six times.

ZEKE
Then try seven.

She tunes the dial, lips pressed white. Tibor leans over the boy, listening to his chest, then sits back on his heels.

TIBOR
Sarge.

ZEKE
How is he?

TIBOR
He's gone.

ZEKE
How long?

TIBOR
A few minutes. I tried.

Zeke nods once. Peels off a glove. Lays a bare hand on the boy's forehead, as though checking for fever. Withdraws it.

WILLA
Sarge, who was he?

ZEKE
A kid from Prague. Karel.

WILLA
Did he have anyone?

ZEKE
A mother. I'll write her.

TIBOR
You wrote the last one.

ZEKE
Somebody has to.

A shell lands far off. The ground trembles. Snow shivers from the angel's wings.

WILLA
Sarge, if we're being honest, I don't think Battalion is coming.

ZEKE
You're not wrong.

WILLA
Then why are we here?

ZEKE
Because they told us to hold the churchyard.

TIBOR
And the churchyard is holding.

ZEKE
Barely.

Willa laughs, short and wet. Tibor smiles despite himself. Zeke looks at the broken bell tower, the stained glass scattered across the snow like spilled paint.

ZEKE (CONT'D)
My grandmother used to say: a bell can't be broken. It can only be silenced.

TIBOR
She sounds wise.

ZEKE
She was a terror.

WILLA
Sarge. The radio. I've got something.

She presses the earpiece to her head. A voice, faint, from the other end of the war.

WILLA
(listening, barely breathing)
All units, cease fire. I repeat, ceasefire in effect at oh-six-hundred.

Willa stares at the dial. Tibor closes his eyes. Zeke sits down slowly in the snow.

ZEKE
Well, then.

WILLA
Is it over?

ZEKE
For tonight.

Tibor takes the boy's hand and holds it. Willa, shaking, unslings the radio and sits beside the Sergeant. Zeke puts an arm around her. Dawn begins to gray the east.

TIBOR
Sarge. May I?

ZEKE
Go ahead.

Tibor draws a small prayer book from his pocket and begins to read, in Czech, in a voice too soft to carry beyond the angel.
